export const FACTS = "COURSE FACTS (from the Agents 101 module pages; use only these, invent no other course content)\n\nThe learner names one challenge they carry at work that is not solved yet, not a task. Module 1 is about the learner themself. From Module 2 on, every module works on that same challenge. Each module hands the agents one more part of the job.\n\n1 Getting Going, 80 min.\nBig idea: Context is how you paint by LLM. What you give the agent shapes what comes back, and you can go very deep with it: your profile, your rules, your judgement on the result.\nExercises: \"Paint by agent with guardrails\" (steps: The boring baseline; Anti-branding, the mirror; List what you hate, then invert it; Say who the site is for; Finish Godin's line with what you believe; Strengths).\nSkills: Engineer context, Write rules for an agent, Judge agent output.\nKey points: Context is the brush: what you give the agent shapes the output. Output got specific because the context did. Guardrails are context you can reuse. Your domain expertise is the test.\nAgents take: An agent writes a page about you, and you catch what it made up. You keep: Everything. You learn what to give an agent. You end with: A page that is you, and rules for an agent.\n\n2 Building Agent Systems, 90 min.\nBig idea: A system remembers, grows, and compounds around the one thing you need to make progress on. Chat can't.\nExercises: \"Name your next big challenge\", \"Build your challenge memory\" (steps: Curate, ingest, build; Approve the plan before the files change; Curate the source plan; Ingest the sources; Build the memory under a plan; Review the plan, then have the memory audit itself).\nSkills: Create agent memory, Create an agent, Schedule an agent, Review an agent's plan.\nKey points: A system compounds around your challenge: each new source sharpens what is already there. A chat starts from zero. A system is persistence plus automation. Three layers in plain text: untouched raw sources, a memory the agent sharpens, a rules file.\nAgents take: Agents gather your sources on the challenge and remember them. One runs on a schedule. You keep: Naming the challenge, approving the plan. You end with: A memory of your challenge that stays current.\n\n3 Multi-Agent Systems, 90 min.\nBig idea: Several agents can work on one problem together. Split the work, run the agents in parallel, and let them hand off through shared files. Three agents search, three more decide. Handoffs are where work gets lost, so check them.\nExercises: \"Name your crux\", \"Three retrievers, one curator\", \"Three minds, one synthesis\".\nSkills: Orchestrate multiple agents, Split work across agents, Debug agent handoffs.\nKey points: Agents work together through shared files: what one writes is the next one's context. Split where the parts differ, run them in parallel, bring them back to one place that decides. Two ways to run a team: separate sessions on shared files, or helpers the main session starts and discards. Handoffs are where work gets lost: open the files where one agent handed to the next.\nAgents take: Three agents search, three more argue, and an answer comes out. You keep: Starting it and reading the answer. You end with: A briefing on your challenge.\n\n4 Security, 105 min.\nBig idea: You can't tell if your agent is safe from its output. Run a check; don't wait for certainty.\nExercises: \"Run and package a security skill\" (steps: Guess first; Run the policies raw; Dictate what matters; Author both lenses; Answer the questions, then sharpen the lenses; Save the skill), \"Audit your agent\" (steps: Load the reusable check; Run the policy audit; Run the agent-risk audit; Mitigate one risk, reassess the residual; Escalate to a person when judgment runs out; Separate prototype from production).\nSkills: Audit an agent for security, Write a skill, Mitigate agent risk.\nKey points: No certainty: assess, mitigate, reassess the residual, decide. Anything the agent reads can carry instructions, a source document included. \"I can't tell\" is a real answer. Mitigations add to security controls, never replace them.\nAgents take: Agents check what they can reach against your company's rules, and what a bad document could make them do. You keep: Choosing which risk to close. You end with: An audit of your agents and one risk closed.\n\n5 Catching Hallucinations, 85 min.\nBig idea: There is truth out there, and your agent's job is to stay connected to it. Where it cannot reach a source, it fills the gap: confident, plausible, wrong. Grounding, and checks you test on your own output, make the gap visible.\nExercises: \"Hallucination benchmark\" (steps: The target and the benchmark; Extract the claim pool; Run the four detectors; Mark five claims yourself; Scorer runs the benchmark; Read the scoreboard and name the surprise).\nSkills: Detect hallucinations, Benchmark AI checks, Build a judge.\nKey points: Grounded means traceable to a specific file, number or quote. Where the sources end, the agent keeps writing in the same voice. Asking the agent \"are you sure?\" returns another fluent answer. A check has to touch the source. The agent runs candidate detectors on your own output; you read the scoreboard and keep the winner. The judge passed one benchmark. It names its own limit: wrong sources, framing and strategic choices stay outside it. Reusable pattern: candidates, benchmark, scorer, winner.\nAgents take: Agents check their own claims against the sources. The best check becomes your judge. You keep: Reading the scoreboard, keeping the judge. You end with: A saved judge that catches made-up claims.\n\n6 Evaluations, 90 min.\nBig idea: Put the Module 5 judge in a loop and the work improves by itself. The writing agent sharpens against the judge's feedback, round after round, while you walk away.\nExercises: \"The eval loop runs itself\" (steps: Score your Module 3 briefing; Start the loop, then walk away; Come back to the notes; Trace what each round changed; The loop improved the briefing without you; Put another judge in the same loop).\nSkills: Run an eval loop, Steer agents with feedback.\nKey points: The loop improves the work round after round with nobody editing. The judge never changes, so a better score means better work. Groundedness protects the floor; steering raises the ceiling. A flat score is information about the judge or the work. Check both. You design the loop and own the yardstick.\nAgents take: Agents rewrite their work round after round against your judge while you are away. You keep: Deciding what better means. Then you walk away. You end with: A briefing that got better with nobody editing.\n\n7 Agents Building Agents, 45 min.\nBig idea: An agent can build the agent someone else needs. That is how you and the people around you learn faster.\nExercises: \"Build an agent for someone else\" (steps: Pick one real person; Describe the gap; Have an agent build it; Judge it, and the agent rebuilds it; Take stock of the compound).\nSkills: Have agents build agents, Improve an agent with an agent.\nKey points: Describe an agent and an agent builds it, then rebuilds it from what the judge finds. The gap someone else hits is the brief for the next agent. Your agent can improve your agent. Each agent built makes the next one quicker. That is how you and the people around you learn faster.\nAgents take: An agent builds the agent someone else needs, and your judge checks it. You keep: Describing what it should do. You end with: An agent for someone else, built by your agents.";
